\section*{Introduction}
Modern video games are increasingly viewed as more than entertainment: they are structured, data-rich systems where learning algorithms can be trained, evaluated, and deployed at scale. As DeepMind notes, ``Games have been used for decades as an important way to test and evaluate the performance of artificial intelligence systems.'' \cite{deepmind_alphastar} This project focuses on the rhythm game \textit{osu!} \cite{osu_mode_wiki}, but shifts the goal from naming pattern types to modeling \emph{reading chunking}: the way consecutive hit objects are mentally grouped during play.

In \textit{osu!}, players do not read every note independently. Instead, they often perceive a short sequence of objects as a unit, such as a line, triangle, square-like shape, or other local arrangement. This project aims to build a deep learning ``map reader'' that predicts chunk structure from \texttt{.osu} hit-object sequences. More concretely, each object will be assigned a chunk identity and a within-chunk position (for example, the objects in one chunk may be numbered $1$-$2$-$3$-$4$, followed by a new chunk numbered $1$-$2$-$3$). This framing is meaningful because chunking is tied to geometry, timing, and local sequence context, which makes it a natural fit for sequence models.

