\section*{Background \& Related Work}
Recent works motivate learning-based approaches for understanding and evaluating structure in games and, more specifically, rhythm-game charts. TaikoNation targets \emph{patterning-focused} chart generation, showing that neural models can learn and reproduce recurring chart motifs \cite{halina2021taikonation}. Mania Archetype also studies the generation of rhythm-game charts, reinforcing the idea that chart structure can be modeled in a way that reflects player experience \cite{wang2024mania_archetype}. Osu2MIR demonstrates that the information from osu-derived beatmaps can be repurposed into a research dataset \cite{liu2025osu2mir}. More broadly, the PCGML literature surveys deep learning methods for procedural game content, positioning rhythm-game charts as a viable domain for learned structure in game design artifacts \cite{liu2021dl_pcg}.

These works mainly focus on generation, timing, or broad chart structure. By contrast, this project focuses on \emph{reading organization}: learning how hit objects should be grouped into small readable units. This makes the task closer to sequence perception than to difficulty estimation alone. If successful, such a model could support beatmap analysis, reading assistance tools, and future studies on how expert players process local geometric patterns.

\section*{Data Processing}
Beatmaps that contain the \texttt{.osu} files can be obtained from osu!'s official beatmap listing \cite{osu_beatmap_listing}. \texttt{.osu} files will be parsed according to the structure of \texttt{.osu} files \cite{osu_file_format}. A preprocess program will parse the two relevant sections for this project: \texttt{[TimingPoints]} and \texttt{[HitObjects]}. To parse, subsequent lines are read until the next header line beginning with [ or end-of-file by iterating through the file line-by-line.

For each hit object, the preprocessing pipeline will compute a feature row containing spatial and timing information such as $(x, y)$ position, $\Delta x$, $\Delta y$, elapsed time, beat-normalized elapsed time, spacing, and local context features. In addition, each row will include a short context window that describes the relative placement and timing of recent objects. This is intended to help the model capture multi-note geometry rather than only pairwise transitions.

The target labels are no longer pattern names such as jump or stream. Instead, the dataset will represent chunk structure:
\begin{itemize}
\item \texttt{chunk\_start}: whether an object begins a new chunk
\item \texttt{chunk\_pos}: the object's index within its current chunk ($1, 2, 3, \dots$)
\item \texttt{chunk\_id}: a running chunk identifier within a map
\end{itemize}

For the current project scope, the provided \texttt{.osu} files are assumed to already contain chunk-consistent numbering through map structure, allowing chunk labels to be derived programmatically during preprocessing. The resulting dataset is then split into training, validation, and test subsets.

\section*{Architecture}
\textbf{Transformer encoder} will be used as the main model. Intuitively, a Transformer processes a sequence by using \emph{self-attention}, which allows the prediction at one hit object to depend on several nearby or moderately distant hit objects in the same clip. This is useful because chunking is not determined by a single note alone; rather, it depends on the arrangement of multiple recent notes in time and space.

Each hit object is represented by a numeric feature vector rather than a discrete word token. These feature vectors are projected into a learned hidden space, enriched with positional information, and then processed by Transformer layers. The model will output per-object predictions, such as whether the object starts a chunk and what its within-chunk position is. A simpler recurrent baseline such as a BiLSTM may also be implemented for comparison.

\section*{Baseline Model}
A reasonable baseline for this project is a heuristic chunking system derived directly from map structure, such as combo resets or other simple parsing rules. Because the project goal is to learn chunk structure from sequential geometry and timing, this heuristic provides a natural point of comparison: it is cheap to compute and may already capture some chunk boundaries, but it cannot adapt flexibly to more complex geometric reading behavior. The learned model will be evaluated on whether it can recover chunk labels more accurately or more robustly than this rule-based baseline.

\section*{Evaluation}
The main evaluation target is token-level chunk prediction. Useful metrics include chunk-start accuracy or F1, within-chunk position accuracy, and exact chunk reconstruction quality on held-out maps. Splits should be performed at the map level rather than the row level so that the model is tested on unseen beatmaps instead of unseen notes from the same map.

\section*{Ethical Considerations}
Beatmaps may include copyrighted assets (audio/backgrounds), so the pipeline will use only \texttt{.osu} text and derived numeric features, and will not share media files. Since beatmaps are community-created, the project will follow osu!'s content usage permissions \cite{osu_content_usage}. Finally, chunk structure can still contain subjectivity, especially if future work introduces human expert annotations beyond map-derived labels. For that reason, labeling assumptions, limitations, and failure cases will be documented, and outputs will be presented as descriptive rather than authoritative.

